    "bodyMarkdown": "## What is pay yourself first?\n\nPay yourself first (sometimes called reverse budgeting or the anti-budget) flips the usual order of operations: **savings comes out on payday, before any spending happens**. Move a fixed amount to savings and investments the moment income lands, cover the fixed bills, and spend the rest freely without tracking every latte.\n\n## Why it works\n\nIt removes willpower from the equation. Savings is not what survives the month; it is a bill you pay to your future self, automated so it happens whether you are paying attention or not. For people who hate categorizing, it is the lowest-effort method that still builds wealth.\n\n## The catch\n\nPay yourself first is deliberately blind. It guarantees the savings number and tells you nothing about where the rest goes, so lifestyle creep, forgotten subscriptions, and a slowly growing dining habit all hide inside 'the rest.' It also assumes your income comfortably covers essentials plus the savings skim; when things get tight, the method has no answer beyond 'skim less.'\n\n## Pay yourself first vs the category methods\n\n[Zero-based](/zero-based-budgeting) and [envelope](/envelope-budgeting) budgeting are maximum-information methods: every dollar watched. Pay yourself first is minimum-effort: one dollar amount enforced, the rest unwatched. [Count-up budgeting](/count-up-budgeting) sits between them, category awareness with a frame that rewards restraint instead of punishing spending.\n\n## How Buoy automates pay yourself first, without the blindness\n\n- **Savings goals are the 'first'.** Set up goals and fund them on payday; rollover overflow from budget categories can sweep into a goal automatically, so underspending pays your future self too.\n- **The rest is not a black box.** Plaid syncs your transactions read-only and categorizes them automatically, so you get the where-did-it-go picture without doing any of the tracking work the anti-budget was avoiding.\n- **Subscription detection** catches the recurring charges that quietly eat 'the rest'.\n- **Count-up categories** cover the spending side: instead of policing yourself, you watch the unspent allowance grow, which is pay-yourself-first psychology applied to everyday spending.\n\nAutomate the first move, keep your eyes open on the rest. Free trial, no card required. [Start your free trial →](/app/register)"
